    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#guatemala-city"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/guatemala-city.jpg" alt="Guatemala City" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#guatemala-city">Guatemala City</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ciudad_de_Guatemala,_CA.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Rene Hernandez, CC BY-SA 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Federal Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#central-america">Central America</a> emerged from the Captaincy General, or Kingdom, of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#guatemala">Guatemala</a>, the Spanish jurisdiction that stretched from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#chiapas">Chiapas</a> to Costa Rica. On 15 September 1821, following <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mexico">Mexico</a>’s lead, an assembly of officials and notables in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#guatemala-city">Guatemala City</a> signed an act of independence from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#spain">Spain</a>. Weak and divided, most of the provinces accepted annexation to Agustín de Iturbide’s First Mexican Empire in January 1822; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#san-salvador">San Salvador</a> resisted until a Mexican army under Vicente Filísola occupied it in 1823. When Iturbide fell, Filísola convened a congress of the provinces, which on 1 July 1823 declared them free and independent as the United Provinces of Central America. Chiapas chose to remain with Mexico. The National Constituent Assembly, dominated by liberals, abolished <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#slavery">slavery</a> in 1824, and on 22 November 1824 adopted a federal constitution modelled partly on those of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#united-states">United States</a> and of Cádiz, renaming the union the Federal Republic of Central America. It joined five states, Guatemala, El <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#salvador">Salvador</a>, Honduras, Nicaragua and Costa Rica, under a federal government seated in Guatemala City. Manuel José Arce of El Salvador became the first federal president in 1825, after a disputed vote in congress.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The federation had perhaps 1.2 to 1.5 million inhabitants, close to half of them in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#guatemala">Guatemala</a>, whose highland population was largely Maya. El <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#salvador">Salvador</a> was densely settled by ladino farmers, while Honduras, Nicaragua and Costa Rica were thinly populated, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#caribbean">Caribbean</a> coast lay effectively outside state control, with the Mosquito Coast under British influence. The economy rested on agriculture. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#indigo">Indigo</a>, grown mainly in El Salvador and traded through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#guatemala-city">Guatemala City</a> merchants, was the chief export, but the indigo trade was in long decline, and from the 1830s Guatemala turned increasingly to cochineal, the red dye produced from insects raised on cactus. Costa Rica began to grow <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#coffee-economy">coffee</a> for export in these years. British merchants, operating through Belize, displaced Spanish trading houses and dominated imports of textiles. Poor roads, few ports and mountainous terrain kept the regions apart, and each state traded more with the outside world than with its neighbours. Society remained deeply unequal, divided between the Guatemala City merchant elite, provincial landowners, ladino townspeople and indigenous communities. The Catholic Church owned large properties and held great influence over the poor, which made liberal attacks on clerical privilege, tithes and the religious orders a source of fierce popular resentment.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#francisco-morazan"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/francisco-morazan.jpg" alt="Francisco Morazán" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#francisco-morazan">Francisco Morazán</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Francisco_Moraz%C3%A1n_Quesada.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Aquiles Bigot, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The federation never built a strong national army. Federal forces were small, and each state raised its own militia, so that conflicts between states, parties and the federal government were settled by rival local armies. The first civil war began in 1826, when President Arce, drifting towards the Guatemalan conservatives, had the liberal head of the State of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#guatemala">Guatemala</a>, Juan Barrundia, arrested. Federal troops invaded El <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#salvador">Salvador</a>, and the war spread to Honduras. The Honduran liberal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#francisco-morazan">Francisco Morazán</a> emerged as the outstanding commander, winning the Battle of La Trinidad in November 1827 and the Battle of Gualcho in 1828, and on 13 April 1829 his allied army took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#guatemala-city">Guatemala City</a>. The conservative leaders, the archbishop and the regular clergy were expelled. Morazán suppressed conservative risings in the early 1830s, but the decisive challenge came from below. From 1837 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#rafael-carrera">Rafael Carrera</a>, a young mestizo swineherd, led an insurgency of peasants and indigenous villagers in eastern Guatemala, using <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> tactics against government troops. Carrera’s forces entered Guatemala City in 1838, and in March 1840 he defeated Morazán’s attempt to retake the city, a blow that ended the federation’s military power. Morazán went into exile, returned to seize Costa Rica in 1842 and was shot there.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#mariano-galvez"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mariano-galvez.jpg" alt="Mariano Gálvez" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#mariano-galvez">Mariano Gálvez</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mariano_G%C3%A1lvez.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The constitution of 1824 divided power between a federal government, with a president, a single-chamber congress, a senate with limited powers and a supreme court, and five states with their own heads of state, assemblies and courts. The federation’s fatal weakness was money: the states controlled most revenue, and the federal government depended on customs duties and the tobacco monopoly, which it often could not collect. Its capital lay in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#guatemala-city">Guatemala City</a>, where federal and Guatemalan state authorities competed for the same resources and loyalties, and the smaller states saw the federation as an instrument of Guatemalan domination. Morazán, elected president in 1830, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mariano-galvez">Mariano Gálvez</a>, head of the State of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#guatemala">Guatemala</a> from 1831, pursued liberal programmes: the closing of monasteries, the end of compulsory tithes, civil marriage and divorce, religious freedom, public education, land grants to foreign colonisation companies and, in Guatemala, the Livingston Codes, a penal code with jury trials adapted from one drafted for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#louisiana">Louisiana</a>. In 1834 the federal capital was moved, first briefly to Sonsonate and then to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#san-salvador">San Salvador</a>, to escape Guatemalan politics. Repeated proposals to reform the constitution and give the federation its own revenue failed, and federal authority depended increasingly on Morazán himself.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#justo-rufino-barrios"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/justo-rufino-barrios.jpg" alt="Justo Rufino Barrios" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#justo-rufino-barrios">Justo Rufino Barrios</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Justo_Rufino_Barrios_Auy%C3%B3n.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown authorUnknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The federation was the only time the five Central American states have been united as a sovereign country, and its failure shaped the region’s history. Its collapse left five small republics, each vulnerable to foreign intervention, from the American filibuster <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#william-walker">William Walker</a> in Nicaragua in the 1850s to the repeated interventions of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#united-states">United States</a> in the twentieth century. Yet the ideal of union proved remarkably persistent. Further attempts followed throughout the nineteenth century, including a short-lived confederation in the 1840s, the Guatemalan president <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#justo-rufino-barrios">Justo Rufino Barrios</a>’s attempt to impose union by force, which ended with his death at the Battle of Chalchuapa in 1885, and the Greater Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#central-america">Central America</a> of 1896–1898. In the twentieth century unionism lived on in the Central American Court of Justice of 1907, the Organisation of Central American States of 1951, the Central American <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#european-economic-community">Common Market</a> of 1960 and the Central American Integration System founded in 1991. The federation’s symbols endure: the blue and white bands of its flag survive in the flags of all five former states, and the five volcanoes of its coat of arms appear on the arms of El <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#salvador">Salvador</a> and Nicaragua. Morazán is honoured as a national hero across the region, especially in Honduras and El Salvador.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#rafael-carrera"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rafael-carrera.jpg" alt="Rafael Carrera" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#rafael-carrera">Rafael Carrera</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Carrerayturcios_2014-06-22_09-46_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unidentified painter, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The federation’s collapse began in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#guatemala">Guatemala</a>. Gálvez’s reforms, especially a head tax, new courts and land grants to foreigners, angered rural communities, and when cholera struck in 1837, rumours spread that the government had poisoned the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#well">wells</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#rafael-carrera">Rafael Carrera</a>’s rising, supported by the clergy and conservatives, broke liberal control of the state, and in 1838 western Guatemala broke away as the State of Los Altos, with Quetzaltenango as its capital. On 30 May 1838 the federal congress declared the states free to organise themselves as they wished. Nicaragua seceded in April 1838, Honduras in October and Costa Rica in November, and Guatemala followed in April 1839, while Carrera crushed Los Altos in 1840. Morazán’s presidential term expired in February 1839 and no successor was elected; he held out in El <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#salvador">Salvador</a> as its head of state, but after his defeat at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#guatemala-city">Guatemala City</a> in March 1840 he left for exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#peru">Peru</a>. El Salvador, the last defender of the union, adopted a constitution as a sovereign republic in 1841, and the federation ceased to exist. Morazán returned in 1842, overthrew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#braulio-carrillo">Braulio Carrillo</a> in Costa Rica and tried to rebuild the union from there, but a popular revolt toppled him and he was executed in San José on 15 September 1842.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Federal Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#central-america">Central America</a> was the only state ever to unite <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#guatemala">Guatemala</a>, El <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#salvador">Salvador</a>, Honduras, Nicaragua and Costa Rica; its liberal experiment foundered on poverty, weak federal finances, fear of Guatemalan dominance and popular revolt, but its ideal of union and its symbols still shape Central American identity.</em></p>

